The '''Australian Institute of Physics''' was established in 1963, when it replaced the Australian Branch of the British [[Institute of Physics]] based in London.<ref name="Webster">[http://www.physics.uq.edu.au/physics_museum/webster.pdf ''A History of the Physics Department of the University of Queensland'' Emeritus Professor H C Webster, 31 March 1977, accessed 6 February 2012] {{webarchive |url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120319095137/http://www.physics.uq.edu.au/physics_museum/webster.pdf |date=19 March 2012 }}</ref> The purpose of the institute is to promote the role of physics in research, education, industry and the community.<ref>[https://www.aip.org.au/About-The-AIP Australian Institute of Physics]</ref> The AIP publishes ''Australian Physics'' (ISSN 1036-3831) since 1963. Every two years, the Institute organises a national congress, the latest being held in December 2024 in Melbourne.<ref>{{Cite web |url=https://aipcongress2024.com/|title=The 25th Congress of the Australian Institute of Physics}}</ref>

{{Short description|Subtopic within human cultural geography}}
{{Geography sidebar}}

'''Emotional geography''' is a subtopic within [[human geography]], more specifically [[cultural geography]], which applies psychological theories of [[emotion]]. It is an [[Interdisciplinarity|interdisciplinary]] field relating emotions, [[Location|geographic places]] and their contextual environments. These subjective feelings can be applied to individual and social contexts. Emotional geography specifically focuses on how human emotions relate to, or affect, the [[Social environment|environment]] around them.<ref>{{cite book |doi=10.1093/acref/9780199599868.001.0001 |title=A Dictionary of Human Geography |date=2013 |last1=Castree |first1=Noel |last2=Kitchin |first2=Rob |last3=Rogers |first3=Alisdair |isbn=978-0-19-959986-8 |chapter=emotional geographies |page=125 |chapter-url={{GBurl|eYWcAQAAQBAJ|p=125}} }}</ref><ref>{{cite journal |last1=Kearney |first1=Amanda |title=Homeland Emotion: An Emotional Geography of Heritage and Homeland |journal=International Journal of Heritage Studies |date=March 2009 |volume=15 |issue=2–3 |pages=209–222 |doi=10.1080/13527250902890746 }}</ref><ref>{{cite book |doi=10.21832/9781845411299-004 |jstor=jj.27195511.6 |chapter-url={{GBurl|o7cIIMLAXXoC|p=1}} |chapter=Performative Authenticity in Tourism and Spatial Experience: Rethinking the Relations Between Travel, Place and Emotion |title=Re-Investing Authenticity |date=2010 |last1=Knudsen |first1=Britta Timm |last2=Waade |first2=Anne Marit |pages=1–20 |isbn=978-1-84541-129-9 |editor-last1=Knudsen |editor-last2=Waade |editor-first1=Britta Timm |editor-first2=Anne Marit }}</ref><ref>{{cite book |doi=10.22459/AP.10.2009.19 |doi-access=free |chapter=Manankurra: What's in a name? Placenames and emotional geographies |title=Aboriginal Placenames. Naming and re-naming the Australian landscape |date=2009 |last1=Bradley |first1=John J. |last2=Kearney |first2=Amanda |isbn=978-1-921666-08-7 |editor-last1=Koch |editor-last2=Hercus |editor-first1=Harold |editor-first2=Luise |pages=463–480 }}</ref><ref>{{cite book |doi=10.1353/book.126955 |title=Shredding the Map: Imagined Geographies of Revolutionary Russia, 1914-1922 |date=2024 | last1=Clowes |first1=Edith W. |isbn=978-1-943208-78-4}}</ref>

Firstly, there is a difference between emotional and [[Affect (psychology)|affectual]] geography and they have their respective geographical sub-fields. The former refers to theories of expressed feelings and the social constructs of expressed feelings which can be generalisable and understood globally. The latter refers to theories underlying inexpressible feelings that are independent, embodied, and hard to understand.<ref name=":0">{{cite book |doi=10.4324/9781315797489 |title=Cultural Geographies |date=2013 |last1=Horton |first1=John |last2=Kraftl |first2=Peter |isbn=978-1-317-75368-1 }}{{pn|date=June 2025}}</ref>

Emotional geography approaches geographical concepts and research from an expressed and generalisable perspective. Historically, emotions have an ultimate adaptive significance by accentuating a non-verbal form of communication that is universal.<ref>{{cite book |last1=Gray |first1=Peter O. |last2=Bjorklund |first2=David |title=Psychology |date=2018 |publisher=Macmillan Learning |isbn=978-1-319-15051-8 |edition=8th |oclc=1007923744 }}{{pn|date=June 2025}}</ref> This dates back to [[Evolution of emotion|Darwin's theory of emotion]], which explains the evolutionary development of expressed emotion. This aids individual and societal relationships as there is the presence of emotional communication. For example, when studying social phenomena, individuals' emotions can connect and create a social emotion which can define the event happening.<ref name=":1">{{cite journal |last1=Everts |first1=Jonathan |last2=Wagner |first2=Lauren |title=Guest Editorial: Practising emotions |journal=Emotion, Space and Society |date=August 2012 |volume=5 |issue=3 |pages=174–176 |doi=10.1016/j.emospa.2012.02.004 }}</ref>

So, emotional geography applies emotional theory to places, emphasising the individual and social presence of it.

